<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philipp Scheidemann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philipp Scheidemann was a German Social Democratic politician. On 9 November 1918, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s abdication was announced, he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">German Republic</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> building. The armistice followed two days later, marking the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann.jpg" alt="Philipp Scheidemann" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1979-122-29A,_Philipp_Scheidemann_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philipp Scheidemann was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> on 26 July 1865, the son of an upholsterer. After his father died in 1879 the family fell into poverty, and he trained as a typesetter and letterpress printer. In 1883 he joined the Social Democratic Party, then banned under Bismarck's Anti-Socialist Laws, and later worked as a newspaper editor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, Offenbach and Kassel. From 1909 he wrote dialect stories under the pseudonym Henner Piffendeckel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Scheidemann entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in 1903. After August Bebel's death in 1913 he took over the chairmanship of the party's parliamentary group with Hugo Haase, and in 1912 he became the first Social Democrat elected a vice president of the Reichstag, though he could not take office until 1918 because he refused the customary visit to the emperor. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he took a middle line, supporting war credits but opposing a victorious peace and advocating a negotiated peace without annexations, which militarist circles called high treason. From October 1917 he was chairman of the Majority Social Democratic Party alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>.</p><p>In November 1918 he concluded that a successful fight against the extreme left was possible only if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> abdicated, and on 6 November he said the party must put itself at the head of the movement or anarchy would follow. From the balcony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">German Republic</a>. In February 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> elected Ebert president, and Ebert asked Scheidemann to form a government. He served as Reich minister president from 13 February to 20 June 1919 and resigned because the cabinet could not agree on accepting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><p>He remained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> until 1933 and was mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> from 1920 to 1925. On 4 June 1922 two men sprayed prussic acid at his face during a walk; strong winds saved him, and the attackers were caught and sentenced to long prison terms. The extreme right and left both made his name a term of abuse for supporters of the Republic. His Memoirs of a Social Democrat appeared in 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Nazis came to power Scheidemann went into exile in February or March 1933, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, then through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1935. He was deprived of his German citizenship on the first expatriation list of 25 August 1933, and his diaries were confiscated and are considered lost. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 29 November 1939, and in 1953 his ashes were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>. His critical writings from exile were published only in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philipp Scheidemann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wilhelm II's abdication was announced on 9 November, <b>Philipp Scheidemann</b> proclaimed the German Republic the same day, and the armistice followed two days later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November 1918 <b>Philipp Scheidemann</b> of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the German Republic at the Reichstag building, the abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II was announced, and Prince Max of Baden handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With revolution spreading, Chancellor Max von Baden announced the Kaiser's abdication and <b>Philipp Scheidemann</b> proclaimed a republic in Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.32,9.5&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Kassel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Philipp Scheidemann"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5505 5703h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5505 5703h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.32,9.5&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
